  "id": "Compound::DB00488 - Disease::DOID:2394",
  "verbalization": "Altretamine is implicated in the treatment of ovarian cancer through its inclusion in alkylating activity, which is a class of drugs that damage DNA and inhibit tumor growth. This pathway is shared with Melphalan and Chlorambucil, both of which are also alkylating agents and are known to treat ovarian cancer. The shared ancestry of Altretamine with these compounds, through the category of Antineoplastic Alkylating Agents, suggests a common mechanism of action that contributes to its efficacy against ovarian cancer. The evidence also highlights that Altretamine may have additional effects, such as causing peripheral sensory neuropathy, which is linked to Paclitaxel, another agent that is used in the treatment of ovarian cancer. This suggests that Altretamine may have a broader impact on cancer treatment, potentially through multiple mechanisms. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of alkylating agents in the management of ovarian cancer, with Altretamine serving as a relevant example of how such drugs may be repurposed for therapeutic use.",
